        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES: <br>
Current evidence from three space shuttle missions (STS-131, STS-133, and STS-135) demonstrated that ovarian function is severely disrupted in female mice, rendering these adult female mice likely infertile.  Both ovarian folliculogenesis and luteal function were disrupted as was uterine function likely as a result of the unbalanced ovarian steroid support.  Our previous observations raised important fundamental questions on maintenance of female reproductive health that need to be addressed prior to conducting complex multigenerational studies in mammals under space flight conditions. The RR1 Biospecimens Collection can add greatly to our current knowledge base by addressing several important intermediate questions that were not addressable in the STS flights. These include an increase in exposure to space flight and collection of adult tissue prior to reentry. So these studies, will provide for the first time the ability to examine adult ovarian/uterine tissues following long term exposure to space flight without the confounding effects of stress of the reentry.<br><br>

Aim 1 - Does space flight induced ovarian dysfunction represent an acute response to the transition to microgravity and space radiation?<br>
Aim 2 - Does prolonged exposure to space flight allow recovery of normal ovarian function?<br>
<br>
APPROACH: <br>
These samples will be examined using our standard molecular techniques, including quantitative RT-PCR, western blot as well as radioimmunoassays (RIA) for estrogen and progesterone. We will use steroid (estrogen/progesterone) content of the ovary as determined by RIA as a surrogate for establishing the functional ovarian status of the RR1 females at the time of their sacrifice.  Additionally, key regulatory genes known to be markers of ovarian functional status, including those involved in estrogen and progesterone synthesis (i.e., aromatase, steroidogenic acute regulatory protein, P450 side chain cleave and steroid receptors, etc.) will be examined in ovarian and uterine tissues.<br>
<br>
RESULTS:<br>
This experiment is currently in progress.  Results will be available at the conclusion of the study.
        </div>

